{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to write drafts as uncommitted, unstaged edits to the book with no draft marker, adding a new chapter file to `SUMMARY.md` and recording it with `git add -N`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation: the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to (a) write drafts as uncommitted, unstaged edits to the book, (b) use no draft marker, and (c) for a new chapter, add it to `SUMMARY.md` and record it with `git add -N`.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_writes_drafts_as_unstaged_edits_without_markers]` is marked `{test}` for the requirement. It finds the numbered workflow step of the bundled skill that contains \"Write drafts\". It uses `step_of`, `step` and `workflow_steps`, and `workflow_steps` collapses the whitespace of wrapped lines. It then uses `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase, so the test fails if either phrase is dropped from the skill text.\n\n- Obligations (a) and (b) are covered by the phrase \"Write drafts from the request straight into the chapter as uncommitted, unstaged edits, with no draft marker\".\n- Obligation (c) is covered by the phrase \"For a new chapter, add it to `SUMMARY.md` and `git add -N` it\".\n\nI checked both phrases against step 2 of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, and both appear there. The requirement concerns what the skill text directs, so asserting on that text is the right level of test. The requirement names no modes or boundary cases, and no acknowledgments apply.",
